    body:
      "When a project starts small, it is tempting to keep all logic close together. That can work for a while, but eventually teams need clear boundaries. I like to split concerns into three layers: UI components that stay dumb, feature modules that own business behavior, and data adapters that translate external APIs into app-friendly shapes.\n\nThe goal is not to create more folders; the goal is to make decisions local. If a screen needs to change how it loads data, that change should not ripple across unrelated features. A practical architecture makes refactoring boring, and boring refactoring is what keeps a product healthy over time.\n\nStart with the smallest structure that supports change, then evolve it when a real pain point appears. Architecture should be a response to complexity, not a prediction of it.",
